.TH EDI-AGENT 1 "2026" "8-Bit Agent 1.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
edi-agent \- lightweight LAN node monitoring daemon and tray application (8-Bit Agent)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B edi-agent
.I command
[\fIARGS\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B edi-agent
is the command-line entry point for
.BR "8-Bit Agent" ,
formerly named EDI Agent \(em a background LAN node monitoring daemon and
PySide6 system tray application for Fedora KDE Plasma (and other
Qt/systemd-based Linux desktops). The command name, config directory, and
systemd unit name are unchanged from the EDI Agent era so existing
installs keep working without migration; only the product's display name
and branding changed. It periodically checks local infrastructure nodes
\(em Proxmox hosts, Plex servers, internal web applications, databases,
and so on \(em either with a plain ICMP ping or a TCP port connect,
and raises a native desktop notification whenever a node's status
changes.
.PP
Full documentation, including a copy of this manual, is published at
.UR http://8bitbunker.org/apps/8bb-agent/latest/guide.html
.UE .
.PP
Running
.B edi-agent
with no arguments (or the
.B gui
command) launches the background tray daemon. All other invocations
are one-shot CLI operations against the shared node registry at
.IR ~/.config/edi-alert-agent/nodes.json .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " name " " ip " [OPTIONS]"
Register a new node. Performs an immediate reachability check before
saving. Fails if
.I name
already exists unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-port \fIPORT\fR
Check this TCP port (a real connect handshake) instead of ICMP ping.
Useful for confirming a specific service is actually running \(em
e.g. 5432 for PostgreSQL, 32400 for Plex, 8006 for Proxmox \(em
rather than just that the host answers pings.
.TP
.B \-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR
How often this node is checked, independent of other nodes. Default
30, minimum 5. The background daemon reschedules itself dynamically
around the shortest pending interval across all nodes, so this value
is honored precisely rather than being rounded up to a fixed tick.
.TP
.B \-\-threshold \fICOUNT\fR
Number of consecutive failed checks required before the node is
marked offline and a critical alert is raised. Default 2 (guards
against a single flaky check over Wi-Fi).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing node with the same name instead of refusing.
.RE
.TP
.BI "remove " name
Unregister a node from the registry.
.TP
.BI "edit " name " [OPTIONS]"
Update an existing node's IP, port check, interval, or threshold in
place, then re-validate it immediately. At least one option is
required.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-ip \fIIP\fR
New IP address.
.TP
.B \-\-port \fIPORT\fR
Switch to checking this TCP port.
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-port
Revert the node to ICMP ping (mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-port\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR
Update the check interval.
.TP
.B \-\-threshold \fICOUNT\fR
Update the alert threshold.
.RE
.TP
.B list
Print a table of every registered node: name, IP, check method,
status, failures/threshold, interval, latency, and last-checked time.
.TP
.B test
Send a test desktop notification via DBus to confirm alerts are
working.
.TP
.B history \fR[\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]
Print the most recent offline/recovery events, newest first. Default
limit 20. History is capped at 200 stored events.
.TP
.BI "webhook set " url
Configure a Discord incoming webhook URL. Every offline/recovery alert
is then POSTed there (as
.BR "{\(dqcontent\(dq: \(dq...\(dq}" )
in addition to the desktop popup, sent on a background thread so a
slow or unreachable webhook never blocks the daemon. In Discord:
channel Settings \(-> Integrations \(-> Webhooks \(-> New Webhook, then
copy its URL.
.TP
.B webhook clear
Remove the configured webhook. Alerts go back to desktop-only.
.TP
.B webhook test
Send a test message to the configured webhook immediately, to confirm
it works.
.TP
.BI "web " "[\-\-port PORT]"
Run just the local web dashboard in the foreground, with no tray icon,
DBus, or desktop session required \(em useful on a headless machine.
Bound to
.B 127.0.0.1
only and never to a LAN-reachable address; there is no option to change
that. Provides the same add/edit/delete/refresh actions as the CLI and
tray Dashboard, calling the identical underlying functions, with a
confirmation step before delete. Default port 7317 (override with
\fB\-\-port\fR or the
.I web_ui_port
key in
.IR settings.json ).
When running the tray daemon
.RB ( "edi-agent gui" ),
this same web dashboard also runs automatically in the background;
select
.B "View Web UI"
from the tray menu to open it in a browser. It cannot deliver live
pop-up alerts the way the tray does \(em pair it with
.B "edi-agent webhook set"
for that.
.TP
.B help
Open the interactive PySide6 manual window
.RI ( manual.py ).
.TP
.B gui
Explicitly launch the background tray daemon. This is what the
systemd user service invokes; running
.B edi-agent
with no command does the same thing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/edi-alert-agent/nodes.json
The node registry: IP, check method, interval, threshold, and last
known status per node. Watched continuously by the background
daemon \(em CLI changes apply without a service restart.
.TP
.I ~/.config/edi-alert-agent/history.json
The most recent 200 offline/recovery alert events.
.TP
.I ~/.config/edi-alert-agent/settings.json
GUI/alert preferences: the selected theme (dark/light), the configured
Discord webhook URL if any, and an optional
.I web_ui_port
override for the local web dashboard (default 7317). Webhook URL is
never printed back out by any command and never written to the log
file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/edi-alert-agent/edi-agent.log
Rotating application log (1MB per file, 3 backups kept) covering CLI
actions, node status transitions, and any errors reading the config
or history files.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/edi-alert-agent.service
The systemd user unit that runs
.B "edi-agent gui"
in the background on login.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A CLI subcommand failed validation or its operation (invalid IP/port/
interval/threshold, duplicate node without \fB\-\-force\fR, node not
found, nothing to update, etc.). The printed
.B [!]
message on stderr/stdout describes the specific failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Add a node checked by ICMP ping:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent add gateway 10.1.1.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add a node checked by a specific service port, checked every 5
minutes, alerting on the very first failure:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent add edi-database 10.1.1.20 --port 5432 --interval 300 --threshold 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Move a node to a new IP after a network change:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent edit plex --ip 10.1.1.150
.fi
.RE
.PP
Review the last 5 alerts:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent history --limit 5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mirror alerts to Discord:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent webhook set https://discord.com/api/webhooks/XXXXXXXXXXXX/XXXXXXXX
edi-agent webhook test
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run just the web dashboard on a headless machine, on a custom port:
.RS
.nf
edi-agent web --port 9000
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR edi-agent-packaging (7)
for how this project is packaged (pyproject.toml, editable installs,
console-script entry points).
.PP
